# ⭐ Phase 19 (`fasi/19-nvidia.md`): Vulkan Video (src/vulkanvideo.c) — only the
#   loader and the headers (libvulkan-dev; `[M]` 1 Oct 2026: 1.4.309 on Debian
#   13, 1.4.341 on Ubuntu 26.04).  The drivers (ICD) are among the Recommends.
# ⭐ Phase 18 (`fasi/18-senza-ffmpeg.md`): no ffmpeg.  Encoding on the GPU
#   with libva directly (libva-dev also brings libva-drm), audio with libopus.
#   `[M]` 30 Sep, in the podman images: libopus0 1.5.2, libva2 2.22, in main.
#   ⛔ Phase 19 (1 Oct 2026, DECISIONI §10.27): OpenH264 and SVT-AV1, the software
#   fallback, are OUT — encoding happens only on the GPU.
#   ⛔ no libyuv: the colour conversion is ours (`src/colori709.c`,
#   fasi/18 §1) — removed on 30 Sep (T10: dpkg-checkbuilddeps asked for it and the
#   phase 18 container does not carry it).
# ⚠ ngtcp2 1.25.0 and nghttp3 1.18.0 are NOT in the Build-Depends: the build
#   container brings them (src/costruzione/quic-statiche.sh), static and at a
#   pinned version (fasi/17 §6.3; D2 closed, DECISIONI §10.6).  The
#   Static-Built-Using field below names them in the finished package (for the SBOM, R24).

Package: remotix
Architecture: any
# Depends: dpkg-shlibdeps computes the libraries from the binary (${shlibs:Depends}).
#   The others are the ones the binary uses at run time and that dpkg does NOT
#   see: pam_systemd and pam_listfile (modules loaded by the PAM stack), usermod (the first
#   connection, figlio.c:1525), loginctl and systemctl
#   --user (figlio.c), the systemd user bus (dbus-user-session), and the PipeWire
#   DAEMON + wireplumber: GNOME/KDE video capture goes through PipeWire (mutter/kwin
#   screencast) and so does the audio of EVERY desktop (suono.c); dpkg sees the LIBRARY
#   (libpipewire-0.3-0) but not the daemon. Stock desktops bring it, but minimal
#   installations do not (T10, 30 Sep: on openSUSE GNOME without pipewire the desktop does not arrive).
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         libpam-systemd,
         libpam-modules,
         passwd,
         systemd,
         dbus-user-session,
         pipewire,
         wireplumber
# Recommends (apt installs them by default; whoever uses --no-install-recommends loses them
#   and `remotix verifica`, T4, will say so):
#   · the GPU's VA driver: without it, NOTHING is encoded (phase 19: no
#     software fallback; the server declares it at startup, and the installer's
#     preliminary check refuses earlier).
#     `[M]` 30 Sep: on Ubuntu 26.04 the Mesa VA lives in mesa-libgallium (which
#     «provides» mesa-va-drivers and va-driver), and va-driver-all only pulls Intel;
#   · labwc, wlr-randr, xwayland: only for XFCE and LXQt (the compositor that REMOTIX
#     starts; wlr-randr for the LXQt and XFCE size cure; XFCE 4.20 under
#     labwc wants Xwayland);
#     ⚠ on a GNOME-only or KDE-only machine they are installed anyway: the metadata of
#     a .deb has no «only if XFCE is there».  One package per desktop is for T4;
#   ⛔ NOT gnome-session (the «vanilla» session from universe): D8 (DECISIONI
#     §10.20) — on Ubuntu REMOTIX starts the STOCK session, `ubuntu`, which
#     is already there (ubuntu-session); the criterion lives in sessione.c, sessione_gnome().
#   · ⭐ phase 19 (`fasi/19-nvidia.md`): the GPU's VULKAN driver —
#     `mesa-vulkan-drivers` brings the Mesa ICDs (RADV for AMD: the Vulkan
#     Video road, tried BEFORE VA-API; ANV for Intel, which does not encode in
#     Vulkan and stays on VA-API).  NVIDIA: the ICD comes with the proprietary driver.
#     dpkg-shlibdeps computes the loader (`libvulkan1`): the binary links it.
Recommends: va-driver-all | va-driver,
            mesa-vulkan-drivers,
            labwc,
            wlr-randr,
            xwayland
Static-Built-Using: ngtcp2 (= 1.25.0), nghttp3 (= 1.18.0)
Description: the Linux desktop in the browser, for several users
 REMOTIX shows a user's desktop (GNOME, KDE Plasma, Xfce, LXQt) in a
 browser, with video and audio over HTTP/3 and WebTransport.  Whoever connects
 logs in with the machine's user name and password (PAM, service «remotix»; root
 excluded by /etc/remotix/utenti-negati) and finds their own session.
 .
 The remotix.service service listens on port 7447 (TCP for the page, UDP
 for QUIC).  The package only carries the pieces, switched off: the service is
 switched on by the REMOTIX installer, the only installation route.  The package
 contains ngtcp2 and nghttp3, statically linked.
